from h34 import *
M=mcq;F=tf;S=short;N=num
b='<text x="10" y="20" font-weight="bold">A centimetre scale: 1 m = 100 cm</text><rect x="20" y="40" width="600" height="50" fill="#fdf2c7" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2"/>'
for i in range(0,11):
    x=20+i*60
    b+=f'<line x1="{x}" y1="40" x2="{x}" y2="75" stroke="#333" stroke-width="2"/><text x="{x}" y="112" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">{i}</text>'
    if i<10:
        for k in range(1,10): b+=f'<line x1="{x+k*6}" y1="40" x2="{x+k*6}" y2="{58 if k!=5 else 65}" stroke="#333"/>'
b+='<text x="320" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">Each small gap is 1 cm; ten gaps make 10 cm</text>'
svgfile('c4-maths-cm-scale.svg',640,155,b)
b='<text x="10" y="20" font-weight="bold">Perimeter: walk around the boundary</text><rect x="60" y="40" width="240" height="120" fill="#e8f6e8" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4"/><text x="180" y="105" text-anchor="middle" font-size="16">garden</text><text x="180" y="34" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14">6 cm</text><text x="320" y="105" font-size="14">4 cm</text>'
b+='<text x="20" y="195" font-size="16">Perimeter = 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20 cm</text>'
svgfile('c4-maths-perimeter.svg',420,210,b)
chapter(4,'maths','measuring-length','Measuring Length',
 ('How long, how tall?','The children in the picture are measuring a tree and a wall. What are they finding out: length, height, breadth, depth, weight or temperature? Length, height, breadth and depth all tell us a length. In this chapter you will measure using metre ropes and centimetre scales, estimate lengths, and find the perimeter of shapes.'),
 [('Words that mean length',['Tall, short, long, wide, deep, high and broad are all words used in daily life for different lengths.','Height, width, breadth and depth all measure length. We use different words in different situations, but they are all lengths.','Weight and temperature are not lengths. They are measured with other tools.','Measure the height of a student with a measuring tape or a metre rope. Other tools can also measure height, like a ruler or a scale.','In Class 3 we learned that length is measured in metres. Most Class 4 children are more than one metre tall. The height of a door is more than a metre, but your arm is less than a metre.']),
  ('The metre, half metre and quarter metre',['We can make ropes of 1 metre, half a metre and a quarter of a metre. Two half-metre ropes make a one-metre rope, and four quarter-metre ropes make a one-metre rope.','Written as numbers: 1 m = 1/2 m + 1/2 m, 1 m = 1/4 m + 1/4 m + 1/4 m + 1/4 m, and 1/2 m = 1/4 m + 1/4 m.','Use the ropes to make lines of 1 m, 5 m and 10 m on the floor by placing the metre rope again and again. Walk, jump and crawl along them to get a feel for how big 5 m and 10 m are.','Measure a long jump with the ropes, and find who jumped the longest and who jumped the shortest. First estimate, then measure and check how close you were.','Estimate the length and breadth of your classroom, then measure it. Practise: 100 metres takes about as many footsteps as you count by walking along it.']),
  ('Guess the length',['A blue whale is about 30 metres long. A bus is about 10 metres long, and a cricket bat is about 1 metre. So we can compare animals using familiar things.','Two blue whales are about 60 metres, which is as long as 6 buses. One whale is as long as 30 cricket bats.','A crocodile is about 6 metres long, so 5 crocodiles are about as long as one blue whale. Two ostriches stand about as tall as a giraffe is.','When we compare to known lengths, we can make a good guess about an unknown length.']),
  ('The centimetre',['A metre is too long to measure small things like an eraser or a grain of rice. We need a smaller unit called the centimetre, written cm.','A metre scale is divided into 100 equal parts. The length of each part is one centimetre.','Look at a measuring tape. The distance between two marks is 1 cm. The numbers show how many centimetres so far. The red bar of 1 cm repeated 10 times is 10 cm.','100 small bars of 1 cm make a metre. So 1 metre = 100 centimetres.','Children often think the 100 on the tape is the number of marks, but it is the number of centimetre gaps. We start measuring from 0, not from the edge of the scale if the scale has a gap at the end.','Chutki can measure her plant every week and note down how many centimetres it has grown.']),
  ('Measuring small things',['Place the zero mark of the scale at one end of the object and read the number at the other end. That number is the length in cm.','Estimate before measuring. A fingernail is less than 1 cm wide, an eraser is more than 1 cm, an ant is about 1 cm or less, a grain of wheat is less than 1 cm and a rajma seed is more than 1 cm.','Roll three toy cars down a ramp and measure how far each goes in centimetres. The car which goes the farthest has rank 1.','In the treasure hunt we walk only on the tiles and go around obstacles. We measure each route in centimetres, and the shortest route is the one with the smallest number of cm.','Trace your hand and measure its length. Then use the hand as a unit to estimate other things like your textbook, a chair or a desk and check with the scale.','If a scale is broken at the start, we can still measure by subtracting. If an object starts at the 3 cm mark and ends at the 9 cm mark, its length is 9 − 3 = 6 cm.']),
  ('Metres and centimetres together',['1 m = 100 cm, 2 m = 200 cm, 4 m = 400 cm, 6 m = 600 cm and 8 m = 800 cm.','A length like 1 m 40 cm is 100 + 40 = 140 cm. A length 4 m 60 cm is 460 cm. 2 m 30 cm = 230 cm and 5 m 50 cm = 550 cm. We can match the wells that have the same depth this way.','Make a height table of children in the class, in cm and in m. 150 cm is 1 m 50 cm. Find the tallest and the shortest child, and count how many are taller or shorter than 1 m.']),
  ('Perimeter: fencing and lacing',['The length of the boundary of a shape is called its perimeter.','Bhola builds a boundary of bricks around his vegetable garden. The number of bricks along the boundary tells him how long the boundary is.','On a dot grid with 1 cm dots, we can count the cm along the boundary. For a rectangle 6 cm long and 4 cm wide, the perimeter is 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20 cm.','Two shapes can look different but have the same perimeter. We find out by counting the length of the boundary of each.','To estimate the perimeter of a desk, a blackboard or a classroom floor, measure each side with a scale and add all the sides. You can draw three different shapes each with a perimeter of 20 cm.'])],
 [('A rectangle is 6 cm long and 4 cm broad. Find its perimeter.',[('The boundary has two lengths and two breadths.','A rectangle has 2 long sides and 2 short sides.'),('6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20.','Add all four sides.'),('The perimeter is 20 cm.','Do not forget the unit.')]),
  ('Change 3 m 45 cm to centimetres.',[('3 m = 300 cm.','1 m = 100 cm.'),('300 cm + 45 cm = 345 cm.','Add the extra centimetres.'),('So 3 m 45 cm = 345 cm.','The answer is in cm only.')]),
  ('An object starts at the 4 cm mark and ends at the 11 cm mark on a scale. What is its length?',[('The scale does not start from zero for this object.','So we cannot just read the last number.'),('Length = 11 − 4 = 7 cm.','Subtract the start from the end.'),('The object is 7 cm long.','Check by counting the gaps.')])],
 ('Explain what a metre and a centimetre are, how many centimetres make a metre, and what perimeter means.',['1 metre = 100 cm','Measure from the zero mark','Perimeter is the distance around','Estimate first, then measure'],['The 100 on the tape is the number of marks','Perimeter is the space inside']),
 ['Height, width, breadth and depth are all lengths.','1 metre = 100 centimetres.','A centimetre is used to measure small things.','Perimeter is the length of the boundary of a shape.','Estimating first helps us check our measurements.'],
 [M('1',1,'How many centimetres make 1 metre?','100',[('10','10 cm is only a tenth of a metre'),('1000','That is more than a metre')],'1 m = 100 cm.'),
  M('2',1,'Which of these is NOT a length?','Weight',[('Height','Height is a length'),('Depth','Depth is a length')],'Weight is measured with a different tool.'),
  F('3',1,'A centimetre is used to measure small things like an eraser.',True,'It is a smaller unit than a metre.'),
  M('4',1,'The boundary of a shape is called its ___.','perimeter',[('area','Area is the space inside'),('height','Height is a length')],'Perimeter is the length around the shape.'),
  N('5',2,'3 m 45 cm = ___ cm',345,'300 + 45 = 345.',[(3045,'The 45 cm is added to 300, not written after 3'),(48,'You added 3 and 45')],'cm'),
  N('6',2,'A rectangle is 6 cm long and 4 cm broad. What is its perimeter in cm?',20,'6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20.',[(10,'You added only one length and one breadth'),(24,'You multiplied, but perimeter needs adding sides')],'cm'),
  M('7',2,'2 m = ___ cm','200',[('20','1 m is 100 cm, not 10 cm'),('2000','That is 20 m')],'2 × 100 = 200 cm.'),
  M('8',2,'The depth of a well is 4 m 60 cm. Which is the same?','460 cm',[('640 cm','The digits were swapped'),('406 cm','4 m is 400 cm and 60 more is 460')],'400 + 60 = 460.'),
  M('9',2,'Two half-metre ropes together make ___.','1 m',[('1/4 m','That is much shorter'),('2 m','Two half metres are one metre')],'1/2 + 1/2 = 1.'),
  N('10',3,'An object starts at 4 cm and ends at 11 cm on a scale. How long is it in cm?',7,'11 − 4 = 7.',[(11,'You read only the end mark'),(15,'You added instead of subtracting')],'cm'),
  M('11',3,'A blue whale is about 30 m long and a bus is about 10 m. How many buses are about as long as one blue whale?','3',[('30','That would be 1 m per bus'),('1','A whale is much longer than a bus')],'30 divided by 10 = 3.'),
  F('12',3,'The 100 on a metre tape is the number of marks on the tape.',False,'It is the number of centimetre gaps. The distance between two marks is 1 cm.')],
 svgs=[('c4-maths-cm-scale','A ruler with 10 numbered centimetre sections and small marks for each cm.','Each gap on the scale is 1 cm.'),('c4-maths-perimeter','A rectangular garden 6 cm by 4 cm with the boundary drawn in red.','Add all the sides to find the perimeter.')],
 keep=['metre','centimetre','perimeter'])
